// PENDING: from an OPEN SigmaHQ pull request. Not reviewed or merged by SigmaHQ yet.
// Pull request: #5878 add: Linux Suspicious Setcap Use with All Capabilities
// Pull request URL: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/pull/5878
// Pull request author: EzLucky
// Change: new rule
// Title: Linux Suspicious Setcap Use with All Capabilities
// Sigma ID: 6d2d94e7-389c-4cf0-b8a5-45d7573dce4d
// Status: experimental | Level: medium
// Author: Luc Génaux
// Date: 2026-02-20 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: privilege-escalation, persistence, T1548, T1554
// Description: Detects the use of the 'setcap' utility with parameters that assign all Linux capabilities to a
//   binary file. This action can be exploited by adversaries to create a completely backdoored
//   binary, enabling unauthorized access, privilege escalation, or the circumvention of security
//   controls.
// False positives:
//   - Unknown
// References:
//   - https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/setcap.8.html
//   - https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/cap_text_formats.7.html
//   - https://dfir.ch/posts/linux_capabilities/
//   - https://juggernaut-sec.com/capabilities/
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/9010263d46c47e333c931932e6eb7ab16b77e67d/rules/linux/process_creation/proc_creation_lnx_setcap_all_cap.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where FolderPath endswith "/setcap" and (ProcessCommandLine contains " all=" or ProcessCommandLine contains " =e" or ProcessCommandLine contains " =i" or ProcessCommandLine contains " =p")
